``` Detection: Pass-the-Hash (MITRE ATT&CK T1550.002)
    Data: Windows Security Event 4624 (successful logon), forwarded from WS01/DC01.
    Logic: PtH authenticates with a stolen NT hash, producing a NETWORK logon (Logon_Type=3) over
    NTLM where the logon process is NtLmSsp. The strong lab signal is a *privileged / interactive*
    account (here domain Administrator) arriving over NTLM type-3 from another host - in a modern
    Kerberos-first AD, a Domain Admin logging on with NTLM to a member host is abnormal and is exactly
    what reusing a hash looks like. Machine accounts ($) and ANONYMOUS are excluded as normal NTLM.
    (The NT hash gives no Kerberos ticket, so the attacker is forced onto NTLM - that is the tell.) ```
index=winlogs source="WinEventLog:Security" EventCode=4624
    Logon_Type=3
    Authentication_Package=NTLM
    Logon_Process=NtLmSsp
| rename Account_Name as acct
| eval target=mvindex(acct, -1)
| where NOT match(target, "\$$") AND target!="ANONYMOUS LOGON" AND target!="-"
| stats count
        values(Workstation_Name) as src_workstation
        values(Source_Network_Address) as src_ip
        min(_time) as first_seen max(_time) as last_seen
        by target, ComputerName
| convert ctime(first_seen) ctime(last_seen)
| sort - count
